# Upstream: giac 2.1.0 usual.cc cos, sin, tan. The half-angle rules (is_half_atrig: e = asin(f)/2, acos(f)/2 or atan(f)/2) return the root r of a square, signed by is_positive(e_) with e_ the evalf of e; for a complex e_ that test is false, so the root was always negated: cos(atan(1+i)/2) gave -0.891156571011+0.0986295774757*i (right 0.891156571011-0.0986295774757*i), cos(acos(2*i)/2), sin(atan(1+i)/2), tan(atan(1+i)/2), sin(acos(2*i)/2), cos(atan(1-i)/2) and sin(asin(i/10^10)/2) the negation too (sin(asin(-1+i)/2) was right: there the root with a negative real part is the right one). Now e is taken again at Digits+20; where that value is real the test on e_ is kept (real arguments keep their results); where it is complex the sign is the one for which +-r agrees with cos, sin or tan of that value, r also at Digits+20 (within 1e-10 relative, the other sign farther than |t|); where neither agrees clearly (r near 0, digits lost in r, e at a cut) the call stays unevaluated and its evalf is the value of e itself.
